-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Per-pixel Overhead:** A naive implementation checking per-pixel ray-segment lists incurs significant overhead. The ERT-table aggregation alleviates this overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Conservatism:** CERT checks a sufficient but not necessary condition for termination. This approximation may reduce the effectiveness of ERT compared to an exact check, but it enables the technique in the scan conversion phase [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Partial Composition:** The efficiency of ERT relies on partial composition increasing the opacity of ray segments, making it more likely to exceed the threshold [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Weak Sharing of ERT Information
-A technique where Working Nodes (WNs) in a parallel system exchange their ERT-table information to increase the accuracy and completeness of termination decisions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Mechanism:** If WN$_a$ terminates scan conversion due to ERT, WN$_b$ (holding cells behind WN$_a$) can also terminate its scan conversion based on the shared ERT-table data [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Parameter:** The frequency of ERT information exchange is set far below the frequency of ERT information updates at each WN to avoid interprocessor communication overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** In evaluations, sharing decreased scan conversion time by at most 43% for Viewpoint A and 50% for Viewpoint B. Effective parallel performance improvement was 7% for Viewpoint A and 30% for Viewpoint B [S1, unofficial].
+A technique in parallel implementations where Working Nodes (WNs) exchange their ERT-table information to increase the accuracy/completeness of termination decisions across the system [S1, unofficial]. If WN$_a$ terminates scan conversion due to ERT, WN$_b$ (holding cells behind WN$_a$) can also terminate based on shared information [S1, unofficial]. The sharing frequency is set far beneath the frequency of ERT information updates at each WN to avoid interprocessor communication overhead [S1, unofficial]. This reduced scan conversion time by at most 43% (Viewpoint A) and 50% (Viewpoint B), with effective parallel performance improvements of 7% and 30% respectively [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The overhead of maintaining the ERT-table is reduced by subscreen aggregation compared to per-pixel checks. The exact complexity depends on the number of subscreens and pixels per subscreen [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a dataset of 307,565 unstructured tetrahedral cells with 62,475 vertices, CERT provided a speedup of 3.86 times in sequential execution on a single Working Node (WN) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables early termination in the scan conversion phase where global ray state is unavailable; reduces scan conversion time significantly [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** It is an approximation (conservative), so it may not terminate as many cells as an exact ERT check. The effectiveness depends on the opacity threshold and the distribution of opaque structures [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When using a scan conversion-based volume rendering method (like Cell-Projection) and parallel execution overhead prevents per-pixel ray state tracking [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The overhead of maintaining the ERT-table is $O(1)$ per subscreen per update, significantly less than per-pixel checks [S1, unofficial]. The scan conversion time is reduced by skipping cells [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires an ERT-table of size proportional to the number of subscreens, plus ray-segment lists per pixel [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a dataset of 307,565 tetrahedra, CERT provided a **3.86x speedup** in scan conversion time in sequential execution (screen resolution 300x100) [S1, unofficial]. In parallel execution, weak sharing of ERT information yielded up to 30% effective performance improvement [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Applicable in the scan conversion phase; low overhead due to subscreen aggregation; effective in parallel distributed systems with weak sharing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Conservative approximation may miss some termination opportunities; effectiveness depends on the opacity threshold and data distribution; requires partial composition to be effective [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When rendering large unstructured volumes with significant opacity, especially in parallel environments where scan conversion is a bottleneck [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the volume is largely transparent (low opacity) or when the overhead of maintaining the ERT-table outweighs the savings from skipped cells [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Introduced in the paper "Cell-projection Parallel Volume Rendering With Early Ray Termination" by Takeo Tomita et al. (2004) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Context:** Developed to address load imbalance in parallel volume rendering by applying ERT concepts to the scan conversion phase, which was previously difficult due to the lack of sequential ray accumulation state [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Usage:** Applied in distributed parallel volume rendering systems, specifically within the CP-PVR (Cell-Projection Parallel Volume Rendering) framework [S1, unofficial].
+Conservative Early Ray Termination (CERT) was introduced in the context of Cell-Projection Parallel Volume Rendering (CP-PVR) [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address load imbalance and inefficiency in parallel volume rendering by applying ERT concepts during the scan conversion phase, which was previously not feasible due to the lack of accumulated opacity information [S1, unofficial]. The method was evaluated on segmented human aorta simulation data [S1, unofficial].
 
